{
  "version": 1,
  "screenplay": "Title: **Midnight In Paris**\nCredit: Written by\nAuthor: Woody Allen\n\n= While on a trip to Paris with his fiancée's family, a nostalgic screenwriter finds himself mysteriously going back to the 1920s every day at midnight.\n\n# ACT ONE\n\n## SEQUENCE ONE\n\n= An opening montage shows Paris in daylight, then rain, then dusk. During the credits, Gil and Inez discuss the city; Gil loves it and fantasizes about living there in the 1920s, while Inez prefers the United States.\n\n.OPENING MONTAGE [[3.25 pages]] \n\nDaylight. Shots of Parisian landmarks and people set the tone. Warm, lively, charming.\n\nIt begins to drizzle. Umbrellas pop open and people in coats run for cover as the rain thickens. On the roads, the occasional car with the windshield wipers on. Empty tables outside cafes and restaurants.\n\nThen the rain stops, making way for Paris at dusk. The glow of streetlights makes the city all the more romantic and magical.\n\nWe see the Eiffel tower. Bright against the night, it's beautiful.\n\n.CREDIT SEQUENCE [[0.75 pages]]\n\nWhite titles on black. Voiceover of Gil and Inez. Gil gushes about Paris.\n\nGIL \nI mean, this is unbelievable. Look at this. There’s no city like this in the world. There never was!\n\nINEZ\nYou act like you’ve never been here before.\n\nGIL\nI don’t get here often enough. That’s the problem. Can you picture how drop-dead gorgeous this city is in the rain? Imagine this town in the twenties. Paris in the twenties. In the rain. The artists and writers.\n\n## SEQUENCE TWO\n\n= Meet Gil and Inez. Gil is a successful Hollywood screenwriter who is drawn by the romance of Paris. Gil's wife, Inez doesn't share her husbands wistful nature.\n\n.MONET'S GARDENS [[0.5 pages]]\n\nGil expresses love for Paris and desire to move there. Inez dismisses it as fantasy and refuses to live outside the US.\n\nGIL (V. O.)\nI mean, look, this is where Monet lived and painted. We’re thirty minutes from town. Imagine the two of us settling here. We could do it. I mean, if my book turns out...\n\nINEZ\nYou’re in love with a fantasy.\n\nGIL\nI’m in love with you.\n\nThey kiss but Inez pulls away to check her watch.\n\n.LE GRAND VEFOUR RESTAURANT [[2.75 pages]]\n\nDinner with his in-laws John and Helen. It's obvious they don't share Gil's enthusiasm for Paris. They're not on the same page on business and politics either.\n\nINEZ\nOh please, let's not get into that discussion yet again.\n\nGIL\nBy the way, it's fine for your father and I to disagree. That's what a democracy is. Your father defends the right wing of the Republican party and I happen to think you've almost got to be like a demented lunatic. But it doesn't mean we don't respect each other's views. Am I right?\n\nBy coincidence, Inez's friends from the States PAUL AND CAROL BATES as her mother brings up wedding plans. They're in France because Paul's going to lecture at The Sorbonne.\n\nDespite Gil's obvious reluctance, they get roped into a trip to Versailles.\n\n.HOTEL ROOM [[1.25 pages]]\n\nOnce in the privacy of their suite, Inez expresses her displeasure at Gil being *anti-social* with Paul and Carol earlier at dinner. Gil admits they're not his crowd.\n\nINEZ\nHe's brilliant. You know, I had such a crush on him in college. And Carol's very bright.\n\nGIL\nI mean he's a pseudo-intellectual. Just a little bit.\n\nINEZ\nUhhh, Gil? I hardly think he'd be lecturing at ***THE SORBONNE*** if he's a pseudo-intellectual!\n\n.VERSAILLES TRIP [[2.5 pages]]\n\nPaul is insufferable as their self-declared tour guide. Inez publicly reveals Gil's struggles with his novel. \n\nPAUL\nI believe Louis moved his court here in 1682. Originally all this was swampland. In fact, if I'm not mistaken, in old French the word Versailles means something like \"terrain where the weeds have been pulled\"...\n\nInez eventually brings up Gil's book qualms. She reveals that Gil's main character works in a nostalgia shop.\n\n.CHOPARD STORE DISPLAY [[0.5 pages]]\n\nInez and her mother gush about her upcoming wedding, especially the diamond ring. Her mother voices doubts about Gil's yearning for Paris. Inez assures her it's just talk.\n\nHELEN\nWell, that frightens me.\n\n.RODIN MUSEUM GARDEN [[1.25 pages]]\n\nA female guide shows the two couples Rodin's most famous statue. Paul insists Rodin's wife was Camille and that Rodin was actually married to Rose.\n\nCAROL\nAre you arguing with the guide?\n\nPAUL\nYes, I am.\n\n.ROOFTOP WINE TASTING / STREET OUTSIDE [[2.5 pages]]\n\nPaul and Carol invite them dancing.\n\nGIL\nI don't want to be a killjoy but I need to get a little fresh air.\n\nInez chooses to go dancing.\n\n## SEQUENCE THREE\n\n= Gil wanders in the rain and at the stroke of midnight is whisked to the past in vintage Peugeot.\n\n.LOST [[3 pages]]\n\nGil wanders the streets until he's lost. Tired, he sits on some steps just as a bell rings. It's midnight.\n\nA vintage Peugeot rolls up the street, stopping in front of him. A man steps out and ushers him in. Disoriented, Gil goes with it.\n\nInside the car, he's sandwiched between women dressed in the style of the twenties. They pour him champagne. Gil drinks up. They hop off at an old building.\n\n.THE FITZGERALD'S PARTY [[3.75 pages]]\n\nGil arrives at some sort of costume party. The man on piano singing *Let's Fall In Love* is a dead ringer for Cole Porter. Gil questions his reality.\n\nA woman with a pronounced Southern accent zones in on Gil. Gil introduces himself as a writer. At that, the woman gives her name—Zelda—and calls for a tall lean. He comes over and shakes Gil's hand, says he's Scott Fitzgerald.\n\nSCOTT\nAnd who are you, old sport?\n\nGIL\nGil—you have the same names as the...\n\nSCOTT\nAs what?\n\nGIL\n(Gestruting)\nScott Fitzgerald and...\n\nSCOTT\nZelda Fitzgerald. The Fitzgeralds! Isn't she beautiful?\n\nScott pulls Zelda into him and they smile winsomely.\n\n.BRICKTOP'S [[1 pages]]\n\nGil and the Fitzgeralds watch a woman dancing. She resembles Josephine Baker. Gil sits in stunned silence. In time he breaks into a smile.\n\n.LE POLIDOR [[5.25 pages]]\n\nGil and the Fitzgeralds jump to another cafe where they introduce him to Ernest Hemingway. Gil is starstruck.\n\nZELDA\n(To Hemingway)\nDid you read my story? What'd you think?\n\nHEMINGWAY\nThere was some fine writing in it, but it was unfulfilled.\n\nZelda is offended and since Scott doesn't defend her, she latches on to a passing man. It's Juan Belmonte.\n\nZELDA \nIf you're gonna stay here and drink with him, I'm going with the toreador.\n\nHemingway advises Gil on writing and refuses to read his novel, instead suggesting they take it to Gertrude Stein. \n\nGil goes to get his manuscript.\n\nHe returns to Le Polidor only to find a laundry shop in its place.\n\nHe's back in the present.\n\n# ACT TWO (A)\n\n## SEQUENCE FOUR\n\n= In a hotel room, Gil tells Inez about meeting the Fitzgeralds and Hemingway, but she dismisses him. At a furniture store, Helen pressures them to buy an expensive chair. Later, Gil returns to the steps and is picked up by Hemingway. In the car, they discuss fear of death. At Gertrude Stein's, Gil meets Adriana and they bond over their shared love for the past.\n\n\n.HOTEL ROOM [[1.25 pages]]\n\nThe next morning, Gil tells Inez how he met the Fitzgeralds and Hemingway. \n\nInez glosses over this, busy getting dressed. They have plans but Gil wants to stay in. Work on his novel.\n\nINEZ\nNo, you can do that later. Mom said we could use her decorator's discount. Get up.\n\n.FURNITURE STORE [[1.25 pages]]\n\nHelen talks up an 18,000-euro chair to Inez and Gil.\n\nGIL\nBut we haven't even found a house yet and we're trying to keep expenses down so I don't have to take any crummy rewrite jobs.\n\nHELEN\n(not impressed)\nWell, you get what you pay for. Cheap is cheap.\n\nThey exit the shop. It's raining and Gil invites them to walk.\n\nHuddled under an umbrella, Helen and Inez head for a car.\n\nINEZ\nGil, no. There's nothing beautiful about walking in the rain.\n\nHELEN\nDon't forget we're taking you to dinner tonight.\n\n.THE STEPS [[2.5 pages]]\n\nWith his manuscript and Inez in tow, Gil returns to the steps where the car picked him up.\n\nTime passes... and nothing. Inez hails a taxi.\n\nINEZ\nIf I'm asleep when you get in don't wake me up.\n\nAlone, Gil wonders what he's doing wrong. As he recounts the previous night, the clock strikes midnight.\n\nAn old car arrives. Gil hops in, greeting Hemingway.\n\n.PERIOD CAR [[1.75 pages]]\n\nHemingway shares his war story. Gil asks if he was ever scared of getting killed.\n\nHEMINGWAY\nYou'll never write well if you fear dying. Do you?\n\nGIL\nYeah, I do. I'd say it's probably my greatest fear actually. \n\n.GERTRUDE STEIN'S [[6.25 pages]]\n\nHemingway introduces Gil to Gertrude Stein as she's critiquing Pablo Picasso's painting of his lover, Adriana. Stein argues he's over-sexualized it. It doesn't capture Adriana's true allure.\n\nSTEIN (TO GIL)\nWhat is your first impression of Adriana?\n\nGil looks to the woman leaning casually against the doorframe.\n\nGIL \nExceptionally lovely.\n\nWith Gil's novel in her hands, Stein reads the opening lines out loud to the room.\n\nADRIANA\nI love it. I'm already hooked.\n\nStein calls Hemingway over to talk business, allowing Gil to approach Adriana.\n\nGIL\nDid my opening lines really get to you that strongly?\n\nADRIANA\nThe past has always had a great charisma for me.\n\nThey discover a shared love for the past, only the time Adriana longs for was *La Belle Époque Paris*.\n\n## SEQUENCE FIVE\n\n= Gil lies in bed with Inez discussing his novel and Gertrude Stein. At a museum with Inez, Paul, and Carol, he challenges Paul's art lecture. Later, Gil attends a Fitzgerald party where he dances with Adriana and rescues Zelda from the river. At a cafe, Adriana discovers Gil is engaged and leaves upset. Gil then explains his time-traveling situation to Dali, Buñuel, and Man Ray, confessing his love for Adriana.\n\n.HOTEL ROOM [[0.75 pages]]\n\nBack in the present, in bed with a sleeping Inez, Gil's still awake whispering to himself.\n\nGIL\nI'm Gil Pender from Pasadena. Cub Scouts. I failed freshman English. But little 'ol Gil Pender has his novel with Gertrude Stein. Oh boy, that girl was so... lovely.\n\n.SAINT-OUEN FLEA MARKET [[2 pages]]\n\nSeparating from Inez and Helen, Gil follows a Cole Porter song to a record store. The pretty shop-worker asks if he likes Cole Porter.\n\nGIL\nI do, I'm a big fan. Yeah, I like to... I like to think that I'm a member of Linda and Cole's inner circle... I'm kidding.\n\nThey share a friendly moment before Inez sweeps in, reminds Gil of another museum date with Paul and Carol.\n\n.MUSEUM [[1.75 pages]]\n\nGil, Inez, Paul, and Carol admire a Monet painting. Paul dominates the conversation. Gil wants to contribute.\n\nINEZ\nShhh! I'm trying to hear Paul say.\n\nOn another floor, they stop at Picasso's painting of Adriana. Paul starts another mini-lecture about it.\n\nGIL\nUhh Paul—I'm going to have to differ with you on this one.  If I’m not mistaken this was a failed attempt to capture a young French girl named Adriana—from Bordeaux, if my art history serves me—who came to Paris to study costume design for the theatre. \n\nINEZ\nWhat have you been smoking?\n\n.RESTAURANT [[1.25 pages]]\n\nInez is finishing up dinner with her parents. Gil has gone on another walk.\n\nINEZ\nThe way the city's lit up at night allegedly inspires him. So... But that's okay because I'm going dancing with Paul.\n\nInez leaves her parents to discuss Gil's strangeness and whereabouts. Helen doesn't support John's idea of having Gil followed.\n\nJOHN\nNo? I'd like to go where he goes every night.\n\n.FITZGERALD PARTY [[3.25 pages]]\n\nGil's dancing at a carnival-themed party. He runs into Adriana who leads him to a carousel.\n\nADRIANA\nIt's from the turn of the century, isn't it beautiful? It's my favorite era.\n\nHemingway, with Belmonte in tow, stumbles in drunk and flirts with Adriana. Gil offers to take Adriana somewhere quieter.\n\nOutside, Adriana confides in Gil that she's broken up with Picasso.\n\n.PARIS WALK [[1.75 pages]]\n\nGil and Adriana take an idyllic stroll. They reach a street lined with sex workers.\n\nADRIANA\nSee anything you like?\n\nGIL\nYeah, I'm ashamed to admit I'm attracted to ALL of them.\n\nADRIANA\nWhen I was in Catholic school, one weekend, my roommate and I, we paid a girl from Pigalle to come and teach us all her tricks.\n\nGIL\nWow... That's... I'm gonna be thinking about that one.\n\n.RIVERSIDE [[1 pages]]\n\nGil and Adriana stop Zelda from jumping into the river. Zelda thinks Scott is unfaithful.\n\nZELDA\nThe more they drank the more he fell in love with her!\n\nZelda tries to jump again but Adriana catches her. Gil gives Zelda a Valium.\n\nADRIANA\nYou carry medicine?\n\nGIL\nNo, not normally. Just since I've been engaged to Inez I've been having, just little panic attacks but I'm sure they'll subside after the wedding.\n\nZELDA\nI've never heard of Valium. What is this?\n\nGIL\nThe pill of the future.\n\n.CAFE [[1.25 pages]]\n\nADRIANA\nYou never said you were getting married.\n\nGIL\nYeah, yeah. I'm uh... You know, it’s still a ways in the future.\n\nADRIANA\nWell, good luck with your book and your wedding.\n\nAdriana leaves, gently turning down Gil's offer to escort her home.\n\n.CAFE BOOTH [[3.5 pages]]\n\nGil meets Salvador Dali, Luis Buñuel, and Man Ray and shares his 'perplexing situation'.\n\nGIL\nI’m from a different time—another era, the future. Okay? I come from the two-thousandth millennium to here. I get in a car and I slide through time.\n\nMAN RAY\nExactly correct. You inhabit two worlds. So far I see nothing strange.\n\nGIL\nWell yeah, you’re surrealists. But I’m a normal guy. See, in one life I’m engaged to marry a woman I love... at least I think I love her. Christ, I better love her, I’m marrying her.\n\nGil confesses about Adriana.\n\nMAN RAY\nA man in love with a woman from a different era...\n\n# ACT TWO (B)\n\n## SEQUENCE SIX\n\n= Inez declines Gil's advances in their hotel room, telling him about Paul's lunch invitation. Gil asks a museum guide about loving two women. John hires a detective to follow Gil. Gertrude Stein informs Gil that Adriana's relationships with Picasso and Hemingway have ended. Gil buys earrings and discovers Adriana's journal expressing her love for him. He steals Inez's earrings but is caught when she returns early; he finds the missing earrings. Gil buys new earrings and is followed by the detective. He visits Stein, learns Adriana is at a wedding, and goes to see her. They kiss, and Gil gives her the earrings before a carriage takes them to the Belle Epoque.\n\n.HOTEL ROOM [[0.75 pages]]\n\nInez eats room service in a towel. Gil wants to get it on but Inez swats him off. She says Paul has invited them for lunch in the countryside.\n\nINEZ\nBut if you don't want to go... Don't go. I'm not missing it because of you.\n\n.RODIN MUSEUM GARDEN [[1 pages]]\n\nGil revisits the guide to ask about Rodin's wife and mistress.\n\nGIL\nDo you think that's possible to love two women at once?\n\nGUIDE\nWell, he loved them both but in a different way.\n\n.DULUC DETECTIVE [[1.5 pages]]\n\nJohn meets with Dubois to have Gil followed. Dubois assigns Tisserant to the case.\n\nAt midnight Tisserant watches from the shadows as Gil gets into a car with T.S. Elliot.\n\n.GERTRUDE STEIN'S [[1 pages]]\n\nStein reveals Adriana left Picasso and has flown to Africa with Hemingway. Stein then gives feedback on Gil's book.\n\nSTEIN\nThe artist’s job is not to succumb to despair but to find an antidote for the emptiness of existence. You have a clear and lively voice— don’t be such a defeatist.\n\n.OUSIDE HOTEL / FLEA MARKET [[3.5 pages]]\n\nInez and her parents load up a car for a weekend trip. Inez informs them Gil won't be joining them because he's writing.\n\nMeanwhile, Gil returns to the flea market. He buys a Cole Porter album from Gabrielle, the shop-keeper girl and an old journal from a bookseller.\n\nEXT. NOTRE DAME\n\nAt Notre Dame, Gil listens to the museum guide as she translates what sound like journal entries.\n\nGUIDE\nI’m in love with an American writer I just met named Gil Pender.\n\nIt's Adriana's journal.\n\nGUIDE\nI know that both Picasso and Hemingway are in love with me but for whatever inexplicable reasons the heart has, I am drawn by Gil... As always in this sad life, he is about to marry a woman named Inez. I had a dream where he came to me and brought me a little gift, earrings they were— and we made love.\n\n.HOTEL SUITE [[4.5 pages]]\n\nGil steals a pair of Inez's earrings and wraps them in a small box.\n\nOn his way out, Inez and her parents return. Turns out John's having chest pain. Helen calls for the hotel doctor.\n\nInez asks why Gil is dressed up. Gil says he's been writing.\n\nINEZ\nYou dress up and put on cologne to write?\n\nGIL\nNo, I took a— I jumped in the shower for a second because you know how I think better in the shower and get the positive ions going in there...\n\nInez spots the box in Gil's hand. She asks if it's a present for her. Gil goes with it, saying it's from the flea market.\n\nINEZ (DOUBTFUL)\nI hope it's my taste if it's jewelry, because that moonstone necklace...\n\nWanting to prove a point, Inez searches her jewelry and discovers her pearl earrings are gone.\n\nINEZ\nDo you think it was the maid?\n\nHELEN\nIt's always the maid.\n\nThey're about to report the theft when the hotel doctor arrives to examine John. \n\nGil *finds* the missing earrings.\n\n.STREET VENDOR / DETECTIVE'S CAR [[0.5 pages]]\n\nGil buys new earrings from a vendor.\n\nIn a car the detective Tisserant watches Gil's ride pull away. Tisserant tails them.\n\nINT. GLORIA STEINS [[0.75 pages]]\n\nStein is speaking with Henri Matisse in her study when Gil drops off his revised manuscript. He asks after Hemingway.\n\nSTEIN\nOh, they've been back for days now. The trip didn't work out. I knew they wouldn't hit it off. It's over. With Picasso, too.\n\nStein says Adriana's alone, attending a wedding at Deyrolle's.\n\nSTEIN\nShe'll be glad to see you.\n\n.WEDDING [[2 pages]]\n\nAt a wedding party, Gil approaches Adriana. He's straightforward.\n\nGIL\nI sense that there's some complicated feelings, maybe—what you have for me.\n\nADRIANA\nAren't you going to get married?\n\nGIL\nEverything's a little up in the air right now. I don't know what is gonna happen.\n\nGil invites her out to talk more privately. They leave together.\n\n.ROMANTIC STREET [[2.75 pages]]\n\nPassing by streetlamps, Gil kisses Adriana.\n\nADRIANA\nWhat are you doing?\n\nGIL\nI don't know.\n\nThey stop to sit outside a cafe.\n\nGIL\nBut I did feel—for a minute there while I was doing it—like I was... immortal.\n\nADRIANA\nBut you look so sad.\n\nHe gives Adriana the earrings he got earlier. She says they're *remarkable*.\n\nBefore Adriana can put them on, a horse-drawn carriage appears. The passengers call Gil and Inez over.\n\nThe carriage takes them even further into the past, to Maxim's during La Belle Epoque.\n\n## SEQUENCE SEVEN\n\n= Gil and Adriana slow dance at Maxim's. At the Moulin Rouge, they meet artists who prefer the Renaissance. Adriana wants to stay in the 1890s, but Gil reveals he's from 2010. They kiss goodbye. At Gertrude Stein's, she and Hemingway critique Gil's novel.\n\n.MAXIM'S [[1.25 pages]]\n\nGil and Adriana slow dance to live music.\n\nADRIANA\nIsn't it amazing? The first time we met, I told you about this place and the Belle Epoque. And here we are.\n\n.MOULIN ROUGE [[6.25 pages]]\n\nAfter catching a performance, Gil and Adriana come to sit with Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec.\n\nPaul Gauguin and Edgar Degas join in. The artists believe the renaissance is the true golden age. Gauguin asks Adriana if she's interested in doing costumes for a new ballet. She's thrilled, until...\n\nADRIANA\nOh my God. I—I don't live here, I mean, I do but I don't but I—\n\nGIL\nIt's best not to go into too many details. We're temporarily passing through.\n\nAdriana asks to speak with Gil alone. They go off to the side.\n\nADRIANA\nLet's never go back to the twenties.\n\nGil reminds her of their friends.\n\nADRIANA\nBut it's the present. It's dull.\n\nGIL\nDull? It's not MY present. I'm from two thousand and ten... I dropped in on you, the same way we're dropping in on the 1890's... I was trying to escape my present the same way you're trying to escape yours to a Golden Age.\n\nAdriana says goodbye, kissing him for the last time.\n\n.GERTRUDE STEIN'S [[0.75 pages]]\n\nStein's happy with Gil's rewrite. Hemingway's read it too and Stein relays his plot suggestion for Gil.\n\nSTEIN\nWell, he doesn't quite believe that the protagonist doesn't see that his fiancee is having an affair right before his eyes.\n\nGIL\nWith?\n\nSTEIN\nThe other character. The pedantic one.\n\nGIL\nThat's called denial. Thank you.\n\n# ACT FOUR\n\n\n## SEQUENCE 8\n\n= Gil confronts Inez about her affair with Paul; she confesses. He announces he's staying in Paris and breaking up with her. Her parents arrive; Gil leaves. John reveals the detective following Gil is missing. Detective Tisserant is seen fleeing from a historical French court after startling its occupants. Gil stops along the Pont Alexandre III and runs into Gabrielle, the record store girl. He tells her he's moving to Paris. They talk, exchange names, and begin walking home together in the rain.\n\n.HOTEL SUITE / FRENCH COURT [[3 pages]]\n\nGil confronts Inez about her affair with Paul. She initially denies it but then confesses after he mentions Hemingway. \n\nGil announces he is staying in Paris and breaking up with her. \n\nGIL\nI'm not going back... I'm staying here and it has nothing to do with, you know, your romantic fling. Paris is Paris. Inez, you and I, it's obvious. I don't think we're right for each other.\n\nHer parents arrive; Gil leaves. John reveals the detective following Gil is missing. \n\nThe detective, Tisserant, is seen fleeing from a historical French court after startling its occupants.\n\nGil exits. John reveals he had Gil followed and that the detective is reportedly missing.\n\nCut to Detective Tisserant entering a period room from the royal French court. Shocked, the occupants call guards on him. Tisserant flees.\n\n.MIDNIGHT IN PARIS [[2.75 pages]]\n\nGil stops along the Pont Alexandre III to admire the Eiffel Tower.\n\nHe runs into the shopkeeper girl from the record store. She's on her way home from a late dinner. He shares he's moving to Paris.\n\nGABRIELLE\nYou know, I thought about you the other day... My boss got a whole new album of Cole Porter songs.\n\nGIL\nAnd that made you think about me? I like to be thought of that way.\n\nJust as he asks if he can walk her home and buy her coffee, it begins to rain. The woman says she doesn't mind the rain.\n\nGABRIELLE\nActually, Paris is the most beautiful in the rain.\n\nThey exchange names. She goes by Gabrielle.\n\nGIL\nIt's a pretty name.\n\nAnd so, their walk home begins.\n\n.CREDITS [[3.25 pages]]",
  "chat": [],
  "notes": []
}